# Dream Pilot Progress — 2026-06-11

One session took the Dreamer from "blocks at node 2, wedges forever, reaper-swept with no reason" to "8 nodes executed, report rendered, clean legible block on a design boundary." Every block now reports its exact node + reason via GET /runs/:runId/status (observability shipped).

## The arc of live runs (work-item:memory-fabric)

- Pilot 001/run 8: blocked node 2 (capture-artifact ref). Wedged/orphaned.
- run 9 (relay timeout): walked 3 nodes incl. relay-backed search/signals; blocked hydrate (needs search ref).
- run 10 (dependency-ref leash): **8 nodes in 11 seconds**, then a downstream op hung (no timeout) → invocation killed → reaper-swept at 17 min. Proved nodes are FAST; the 20-min wall-clock was 11s work + driver pathology.
- run 11 (one-step-per-alarm): REGRESSED to 1 node — single-step's post-pause armAlarmAt(now) does not promptly re-fire in live workerd (passes fake-DO tests). Reverted DO to whole-run; single-step stays available via driveMode for later live debugging (wrangler tail).
- run 12 (whole-run + op-timeouts): 7 nodes; clean block at capture-report-artifact (leash fell to plan.machine ref, JSON read failed).
- run 13 (report-ref + media-type tolerance): **8 nodes** — capture-run, mine-signals, search-memory, hydrate-receipts, correlate-evidence, propose-refinements, render-hitl-report, capture-report-artifacts. **The report renders.** Clean block at hitl-decision-seed.

## The wall: post-review nodes in the initial dream (DESIGN, not a leash)

run 13's block: hitl-decision-seed needs a `memory.hitl-decision.v1` artifact — a HUMAN's accept/hold/reject decision. The initial dream has no human decision yet (you haven't read the report). The planner generated a linear chain that includes post-review nodes (hitl-decision-seed, hitl-follow-up-run-request) which structurally cannot run before review.

This is the same thing Joel named earlier: the planner produces a fixed LINEAR CHECKLIST including nodes that don't belong in the initial pass, because it has no workflow-DESIGN vocabulary and does not read kernel content. The dream-report-canon wants the dream to produce a DRAFT seed (submitted:false), but the node requires a real decision as input — a contradiction the planner can't resolve without guidance.

## Two ways forward (Joel's call)

1. **Leash hitl-decision-seed to draft-from-proposals** — when no human decision exists, derive a DRAFT decision-workflow-seed from the refinement-proposals/report (per canon "submitted:false, planner input for next run"). A node-behavior change, not just ref-wiring. Gets the initial dream to `captured` mechanically.
2. **Reshape the workflow (the real fix)** — teach the planner (via a `workflow` skill in the kernel it actually READS) the right dream shape: explore → correlate → propose → report → STOP for review. hitl-decision-seed + follow-up belong in a SEPARATE post-acceptance workflow, not the initial dream. This is the M4 kernel direction: kernels shape how the planner designs, not just ride pinned.

The dream's CORE deliverable — a rendered HITL report from real correlated transcript evidence — now works end to end. The finish line is a workflow-shape decision, not another leash.

## Infra shipped this session (all on m1-memory-fabric-carve)

Auth on /runs, idempotent leases, skip-to-done guard, M2.5 durable execution (checkpoint/resume/reaper/watchdog, async 202+poll), planner conformance (leash+advertise+validate+observe), dependency-ref-by-nodeType resolution, relay + op timeouts, blocker observability, the live monitor UI (board + per-run + live dynamic-workflow D2 chart). single-step-per-alarm exists but is whole-run-by-default pending a live workerd alarm re-fire debug.

## Run 14: the dream thinks, real data breaks the driver (2026-06-11 PM)

The "dream that thinks" pipeline shipped + deployed (worker 2fc9e926): transcript-source env fix (verifier ran the live joelclaw CLI: 152 real hits), deterministic primary-source criticality backstop, kernel-skill consumption path, two real authored kernel skills on claw-kernel, an agentic propose-refinements node (real lane, receipt-bound, hallucination-dropped, agentic/mechanical labeled). Verifier rated all six axes REAL with file:line proof. Commits c1738e6..5324675 on m1-memory-fabric-carve, 315 tests.

Run 14 (run-live-…566a66dd) outcome: REAPED at node 3. capture-run + capture-generated-artifacts ran (local, fast), then node 3 (search/signals against the REAL JoelClaw index, 152 hits + hydration) is too heavy for one Worker invocation, gets killed, parks at checkpoint 2, reaper sweeps before node 3 checkpoints. The empty-fallback runs did 8 nodes in 11s precisely because there was no data to process; real data made the retrieval nodes heavy.

Two bounded infra blockers now clearly identified (NOT architecture — the thinking layers are done):
1. NODE-BUDGET BOUNDING (new, the real blocker): search/signals/hydrate/correlate must cap their work (maxHits/maxReceipts/correlation edges, and likely stream/paginate) to fit one Worker invocation. 152 hits + per-receipt hydration + correlation exceeds it. The op-timeouts bound a single fetch (30s) but not the cumulative node work. This only surfaced because the dream finally has real data.
2. SINGLE-STEP DRIVER (known): each node needs its own fresh invocation budget + immediate re-arm so a heavy node is not racing the reaper on the slow whole-run watchdog cadence. single-step exists (driveMode) but its live workerd alarm(now) re-fire stalls — needs wrangler tail to debug, not deploy-and-pray. Whole-run cannot distinguish a long-but-alive drive from a killed one without a long staleness timeout, which is why its resume is slow.

Next session start here: (1) bound the retrieval/correlation nodes to an invocation budget; (2) debug single-step live re-fire via wrangler tail. With both, a heavy real-data dream walks node-by-node to captured and self-publishes. The intelligence is built; the carrier is the remaining work.

## Run 15: the carrier's last health bar — poison-record starvation (2026-06-11 PM)

Both Run-14 blockers are now fixed in code, plus a third that only surfaced once the first two stopped masking it. The intelligence layer was never the boss; the carrier was, and this is its final wound.

**The four carrier commits (all on m1-memory-fabric-carve):**
- `aee7ebb` — single-step DO drive + node-budget leash: one bounded node per fresh Worker invocation, with `maxHits`/`maxReceipts`/correlation-edge caps so a real-data retrieval node fits one invocation budget (Run-14 blocker #1, BOUNDED).
- `49787a4` — phantom-safe eviction watchdog: the DO can no longer go dark when an invocation is killed mid-drive; staleness is measured against a marker the watchdog can trust, so a long-but-alive drive isn't confused with a corpse (Run-14 blocker #2, the slow whole-run resume pathology).
- `d919197` — run store provisioned get-or-create across re-drives: a re-driven run re-attaches to its existing Artifacts repo (ALREADY_EXISTS → get + mint token) instead of throwing, so a second drive of the same run can't die on store provisioning.
- `6e44040` — **drain D1-terminal poison records (the diagnosis that took the boss to one bar).**

**The poison-record diagnosis.** `CloudflareWorkflowCapsuleSupervisor` is ONE Durable Object, keyed by `namespace.idFromName(input.workItemId)`. Every Dreamer run shares `workItemId: "work-item:memory-fabric"`, so a single DO drives ALL runs from ONE `driveQueuedRuns` loop. When a drive threw (e.g. planner "Agent lane already completed; refusing duplicate execution", `adapter_unavailable`), `driveOneQueuedRun` returned `"failed"` — and only `outcome === "terminal"` deleted the run-start record. A failed drive KEPT the record. The reaper (`reapStuckRunsForWorkItem`) marked the run `blocked` in D1 but NEVER deleted the DO run-start record. So terminal-in-D1 runs left immortal records in DO storage. `storage.list` is lexicographic, so the older terminal records sort first and re-drive every alarm forever, burning the alarm wall-clock before a newer run is ever reached. That's the Run-15 phantom: a fresh run gets `202 accepted` but later reads `run_not_found` because it was evicted before it was ever driven — starved by poison.

**The fix.** `driveQueuedRuns` now reads authoritative status from D1 per parked run (`createCloudflareWorkflowRunStatusReader({ d1 })`); if the run is in `TERMINAL_RUN_STATES` (`blocked`/`captured`) it deletes the run-start record + driving marker and `continue`s WITHOUT driving. The front door is built lazily on the first run that actually needs driving, so a pure poison-drain alarm never constructs a Sandbox/Artifacts front door (and a flaky binding can't throw before poison is retired). Existing poison drains on the next alarm; future terminal records never re-drive. The DO consumer now does what Bellemare names below: identify and discard the duplicates it already processed.

> "Any effectively once consumer must either identify and discard duplicates, perform idempotent operations, or consume from event streams that have idempotent producers." — Building Event-Driven Microservices, Adam Bellemare

Tests: typecheck clean; `cloudflare-run-async-driver` 11/11 incl. a new regression ("retires a D1-terminal poison record without driving it and drives the fresh run behind it" — poison sorts first, fake D1 reports it `blocked`, asserts only the fresh run is driven and the poison marker is cleared); reaper + artifacts + monitor-reads 20/20; lefthook (oxfmt/oxlint/tsgo) all green.

**Where the boss still stands (one bar, human-gated).** The carrier code is done and test-proven. The remaining deliverable — a LIVE heavy-real-data run walked node-by-node to `captured` over REAL transcripts — requires redeploying the worker WITH the memory-relay binding so the retrieval nodes read the real JoelClaw index. `scripts/workflow-app-deploy-and-seed.mjs` hard-refuses to deploy `MEMORY_RELAY_BASE_URL` without the exact owner sign-off phrase ("exposing JoelClaw/Typesense over a new network boundary"). `MEMORY_RELAY_BASE_URL` is present in env; the sign-off is NOT. So the deploy script throws entirely — a no-relay deploy would be a hollow empty-fallback run (the exact thing Run-13 already did in 11s), not the real-data walk the directive asks for. This is a genuine human-authorization boundary the loop cannot self-satisfy; it is not a code bug. The finish-him move is the owner's: provide the sign-off phrase, then deploy and walk the run live (not deploy-and-pray) via `/runs/:runId/durability`.

## Run 16: the fifth carrier wound — re-drive re-invoked the one-shot planner (2026-06-12)

The poison-drain (Run 15) stopped *terminal* records from re-driving forever. But a **non-terminal** re-drive — a single-step paused run rearming, or the DO alarm re-firing a mid-flight run — still re-entered `run()` from the top, and `run()` re-created the outer safety envelope fresh with no terminal short-circuit. So the planner phase ran again on every drive, re-invoking the ~213s one-shot Pi planner lane. The lane's own `already-completed` admission guard then threw, surfacing as a spurious `adapter_unavailable` block (the specific corpse: `dream-live-20260612T041535Z`). Same boss, different wound: a side-effecting one-shot was being replayed on retry.

**The fix (`abc7b10`).** A new `resolvePinnedDynamicWorkflow` routes the planner phase through `loadExistingPinnedPlan`. The first drive pins `run/plan.json`, which already embeds the machine, harness, verification-contract, and planner-lane receipt. Any later drive reconstructs the full `PinnedDynamicWorkflow` from those embedded artifacts and never touches the planner. Chosen over enriching the lane's `already-completed` decision: less surface area, and the planner receipt is fully recoverable from `planDocument.plannerLane`. Extracting the branch also dropped `run()`'s cyclomatic complexity back under the lint ceiling for free.

Regression: a single-step drive walks the same run `paused → paused → captured` and asserts the injected planner fired **exactly once** across all three drives. Full suite green — 328 tests, both oxlint configs + tsgo clean, lefthook clean on commit.

> "A microservice may process the same data multiple times, say due to a consumer failure and subsequent recovery... The processing logic will be executed each time the event is processed, including any side effects that the code may create, such as publishing data to external endpoints or communicating with a third-party service." — Building Event-Driven Microservices, Adam Bellemare

**Where the boss stands now.** Every known carrier wound is closed in code and test-proven (aee7ebb single-step+budget, 49787a4 watchdog, d919197 run-store get-or-create, 6e44040 poison-drain, c8683ef lane EXIT-trap, abc7b10 re-drive idempotency). The remaining deliverable is unchanged and unchanged in its gate: a LIVE heavy-real-data run walked node-by-node to `captured` over REAL transcripts requires the relay-bound deploy, which `scripts/workflow-app-deploy-and-seed.mjs` refuses without the owner sign-off phrase. The phrase is knowable but the loop asserting it would defeat the gate's entire purpose — the authorization is the owner's to give, not the agent's to read. Finish-him is one human action away: set `MEMORY_RELAY_APPROVAL_SIGNOFF` in `.env.local` (or pass `--memory-relay-approval-signoff=…`), then deploy and walk live.

## Run 17: the sixth carrier wound — abc7b10's reload reads a worktree that never pulls (2026-06-12 PM)

Owner signed off, relay-bound worker redeployed (worker-deploy exit 0, fix LIVE), live heavy run fired and walked node-by-node via `/runs/:id/{status,events,durability}`. It still **blocked** with the exact fifth-wound signature — `adapter_unavailable`: "Dynamic workflow planner failed: Agent lane already completed; refusing duplicate execution: lane:planner:dream-live-20260612T1750Z". abc7b10 did NOT close it. The live receipts say why, and it is one layer deeper than abc7b10 assumed.

**The receipts (dream-live-20260612T1750Z).** `/events` returns 9 events; `eventIndex` is commit order, `at` is generation time, and they invert — proving two drives. Reordered by `at`: a first drive **planned, pinned the plan (planHash e9525710…), loaded the generated harness, and executed a real Dream node** — `joelclaw.memory.capture-run` with outputRefs at 17:52:01.028. `/durability` confirms the inner checkpoint is intact and resumable: `completedStepIds: ["capture-run"]`, `outputArtifactRefs: ["artifact://piwf-dream-live-20260612t1750z/runs/dream-live-20260612T1750Z/outputs/capture-run.json"]`, persistedAt 17:52:01.123Z. Then a **second drive** cold-started the whole outer envelope (resolvingCapsule@17:52:01.835 → pinningPackages → planningDynamicWorkflow@17:52:04.608 → **blocked**@17:52:04.705) and the block stomped the good in-progress state. The brain works — it read and captured real data. The carrier re-planned a run that was already executing.

**Why abc7b10's reload returned null (the real wound).** The artifact store is git-backed (`CloudflareArtifactsGitStore`). `writeText` commits and **pushes to the per-run remote**. But the default read path (`readText` with no `artifactCommitSha`) calls `ensureWritableWorktree`, which `gitInit`s an **empty** in-memory worktree and **never clones or pulls the remote** (`cloudflare-artifacts-store.ts:269-281`). `provisionCloudflareArtifactsRunStore` builds a **fresh store with a fresh empty `GitMemoryFS` on every drive** (the re-entrant startRun, `:350-353`). So drive 2's `loadExistingPinnedPlan` (`workflow-app.ts:3461`) reads `run/plan.json` from a worktree that never received the pushed plan → ENOENT → `catch { return null }` → falls through to the planner → `already-completed` → block. The only read path that sees remote content is `readTextAtCommit` (`:283-307`), which *clones*, and it requires a commit sha abc7b10 doesn't have. **abc7b10's "idempotent planner phase on re-drive" is a no-op across DO instances** — its reload cannot see the pushed plan. The lane's strongly-consistent admission controller (DO storage) *knows* planning completed; that knowledge is discarded as a block instead of used to reload.

The inner durable state is intact and resumable — close the planner re-invocation and a re-drive resumes from `capture-run` and walks forward. The fix is a read-consistency fix to the git-backed store, surfaced to Joel before swinging (it is the foundational I/O path with an empty-remote edge case).

## Run 18: captured over real transcripts, with Wzrrd published (2026-06-12 PM)

The silent clone failure is now root-caused and fixed. The carrier wound was not "planner stubbornness"; it was read consistency. A fresh per-drive `CloudflareArtifactsGitStore` reattached to the existing run repo with a fresh in-memory worktree, then `readText` initialized an empty repo instead of cloning/pulling the remote. `loadExistingPinnedPlan` swallowed that read failure as "no pinned plan", so every redrive fell back into the one-shot planner and the planner lane's `already-completed` guard surfaced as the misleading blocker.

The production fix is `seedFromRemote` on reattached run stores: brand-new repos still init empty, but `ALREADY_EXISTS` repos clone the remote before first read/write. The Cloudflare Artifacts response also sometimes returns JS RPC property wrappers instead of plain strings, so the store now reconstructs `remote`, `name`, and `defaultBranch` from account/namespace/repo fields when needed. `loadExistingPinnedPlan` only swallows real missing-artifact reads now; clone/auth/store failures stay loud.

Walking the live run exposed three more carrier bugs after the clone wound closed:

- Verifier prompts were passed through `LANE_PROMPT`, eventually hitting OS argument/env limits. The sandbox lane now writes the prompt to a sandbox file and passes only `LANE_PROMPT_SOURCE_PATH`.
- Verifier branch retries could hit non-fast-forward on the existing `verifier` branch. The lane command now reattaches to `origin/$LANE_BRANCH` before committing and tolerates no-op commits.
- Wzrrd delivery could darken after verifier acceptance. The broker now avoids a redundant Wzrrd payload reread, persists the authoritative D1 lease row before optional lease receipt artifacts, non-terminal duplicate `POST /runs` re-arms the supervisor, and D1 status projection happens before best-effort capsule event append. That made the publish tail recoverable instead of wedged behind a secondary DO event append.

Final live receipt: `run-live-20260613T000533208Z-ebf1f5c5` reached `captured` at `2026-06-13T00:52:39.858Z`. It executed all 15 generated workflow steps over the trusted JoelClaw memory relay, verifier accepted the generated workflow outputs, Wzrrd delivery status was `published`, and the noindex review page is live at `https://run-live-20260613t000533208z-ebf1f5c5.wzrrd.sh/`.

Deploy receipt: final worker version `1bba8539-7b5e-47b4-9464-b7ffbee8a394`, deployed with the owner signoff phrase and `WZRRD_API_TOKEN` bound. Local validation after the last code patch: `pnpm check`, `pnpm typecheck`, `pnpm typecheck:tsc`, and `pnpm exec vitest run` passed (339 tests).

## Phase 1b: lifecycle-faithful fakes catch the carrier wounds (2026-06-12)

The Phase 1b test harness now models the wound that Run 17 exposed instead of letting process memory lie. `tests/integration/lifecycle-faithful-fakes.ts` defines a shared fake Artifacts remote and fresh per-drive stores: a brand-new drive starts empty, while a reattached drive must explicitly `seedFromRemote` to read prior committed artifacts. `tests/integration/workflow-app.test.ts` includes the run-17 reproduction: first drive writes `run/plan.json`, an unseeded redrive gets `ENOENT`, and a seeded redrive reads the plan.

The Dream chaos-resume suite drives the generated Dream shape with a fresh `WorkflowApp` and fresh artifact store per simulated alarm. It kills/redrives after the submit/admission boundary, after plan pinning, after verification, before capture finalize, and across every Dream node boundary. Assertions pin the important invariant: terminal `captured`, planner invoked exactly once, every Dream node executed exactly once, and every post-first drive seeded from the shared remote. The suite also carries the required `test.fails` for stale terminal status stomping with the comment `// flips when phase1a drive ledger merges`; that behavior belongs to Phase 1a write fencing, not this branch.

`tests/integration/cloudflare-run-async-driver.test.ts` now has an explicit fake DO lifecycle harness: every alarm uses a fresh `CloudflareWorkflowCapsuleSupervisor` instance over the same fake storage, proving checkpoints/run-start records survive eviction while instance fields do not.

Validation receipt for this branch: focused lifecycle tests passed (`workflow-app`, `cloudflare-run-async-driver`, `cloudflare-artifacts-store-clone`), and `pnpm verify` passed. Full `pnpm test:integration` is blocked in this restricted Codex sandbox by pre-existing environment issues: local relay tests cannot bind `127.0.0.1` (`EPERM`) and the workflow monitor prototype import expects generated SvelteKit `.svelte-kit/tsconfig.json`.
